`,e.jsxs("figure",{"data-wide":!0,children:[e.jsx("a",{href:"/diagrams/switchyard-architecture.webp",target:"_blank",rel:"noopener",children:e.jsx("img",{src:"/diagrams/switchyard-architecture.webp",width:"1120",height:"1024",loading:"lazy",decoding:"async",alt:"Switchyard architecture. Clients send OpenAI-compatible requests to the router, a single Go binary. Inside it, admission control, the prefix keyer, the routing policy, and the streaming proxy form the request path; the policy reads a prefix index and a load tracker; a KV event subscriber updates the index from vLLM's events; a health checker probes the replicas. The proxy streams to one of four vLLM replicas on one RTX 4090."})}),e.jsx("figcaption",{children:"Fig. 1. The request path (numbered) and the control plane. Select the image for full size."})]}),`
`,e.jsx(t.p,{children:"Each request is reduced to a canonical byte form (model, tools, messages) and hashed in fixed-size blocks, each hash covering everything before it, so requests that share a prefix share leading hashes. The router remembers which blocks it sent where, within each replica's KV capacity, with LRU eviction and a TTL."}),`
`,e.jsxs(t.p,{children:["An optional precise mode goes further: the Go code reproduces vLLM's own ",e.jsx(t.code,{children:"sha256_cbor"})," block hashes bit for bit, verified against vLLM's functions and against a live engine, and keeps the index in sync from the engines' KV cache events over ZeroMQ."]}),`
